{"conjecture": "EJ = K P_y, i.e. segment EJ equals segment KP_y (both equal FP_y = FP_x^2/FV), for every P_x on h1.", "reason": "Use a frame with F as origin and line j as the x-axis. GF is a diameter, so j is the tangent at F and angle GEF = 90°. V is where the tangents at F and E meet, so VF = VE. In right triangle FED (D = GE ∩ j), the point V on the hypotenuse FD with VF = VE is the midpoint, so VF = VE = VD and FD = 2·FV. Line e is perpendicular to j at D, and h1 is parallel to e through V, so h1 is the midline of the strip between F and e. Hence K = 2·P_x on ray FP_x, which makes P_x the midpoint of FK. Line m is perpendicular to FK at its midpoint P_x, so P_y lies on the perpendicular bisector of FK and P_yF = P_yK. Because P_yK is parallel to j, the isosceles triangle F P_y K gives angle(P_yFK) = angle(P_yKF) = angle(FK, j). So FK bisects the angle between j and FP_y. With θ = angle(FP_x, j), this gives FV = FP_x cos θ and FP_x = FP_y cos θ. Therefore FP_x^2 = FV·FP_y, the mean-proportional relation. Next, H is the foot of P_y on j, and HJ is parallel to DE. Triangle VDE is isosceles (VD = VE), so the parallel through H cuts off an isosceles triangle VHJ with VH = VJ. Thus EJ = EV + VJ = VD + VH = HD. Finally, HD is the distance from P_y to e measured parallel to j, which is P_yK. So EJ = KP_y = FP_y = FP_x^2/FV. Numerical check: EJ ≈ 11.345 and KP_y ≈ 11.345.", "closes_goal": true}
```

