{"conjecture": "EJ = KP_y (and both equal FP_y = FP_x^2/FV)", "reason": "Line j is the tangent at F, since j is parallel to f and GF is a diameter. So V = g ∩ j has equal tangent lengths: VE = VF. The chord GE meets j at D. Triangle GFD has a right angle at F, and angle GEF = 90°. The tangent-chord angle then gives angle VED = angle VDE, so VD = VE = VF, which makes V the midpoint of FD. Because h1 is parallel to e and passes through V, the intercept theorem makes P_x the midpoint of FK. Line m is perpendicular to FK at P_x, so it is the perpendicular bisector of FK. Therefore P_yF = P_yK, and P_y lies on the parabola with focus F and directrix e. Since KP_y is parallel to FV, angle P_yFK = angle P_yKF = angle P_xFV. The right triangles FVP_x (right angle at V) and FP_xP_y (right angle at P_x) are therefore similar, which gives the mean proportional FP_x^2 = FV·FP_y. In coordinates with FV = a and VP_x = t, this is FP_y = a + t^2/a = KP_y. Next, KP_yHD is a rectangle, so KP_y = DH = DV + VH. The line HJ is parallel to DE, so triangle VHJ is similar to the isosceles triangle VDE, and VJ = VH. V lies between E and J on g, so EJ = EV + VJ = VD + VH = DH. Hence EJ = KP_y = FP_y = FP_x^2/FV for every P_x. Numerically EJ = KP_y = 11.345.", "closes_goal": true}
