{"conjecture": "EJ = KP_y (both equal FP_y = FP_x^2 / FV)", "reason": "Numerically EJ ≈ KP_y ≈ FP_y ≈ 11.345, and FP_x^2 ≈ 26.20 ≈ FV·FP_y. Why: (1) F is the antipode of G, so j (parallel to f) is the tangent at F. V is where the tangents at E and F meet, so VE = VF and AV ⊥ EF. Since GE ⊥ EF (angle in a semicircle), GE ∥ AV. A is the midpoint of GF, so D = GE∩j satisfies FD = 2·FV. (2) h1 and e are both perpendicular to j, at V and D. By Thales, FK = 2·FP_x, so P_x is the midpoint of FK. m is perpendicular to FK at P_x, so m is the perpendicular bisector of FK, and P_y on m gives P_yK = P_yF. (3) Take coordinates F=(0,0), j as the x-axis, V=(v,0), P_x=(v,t). Then K=(2v,2t) and P_y=((v^2−t^2)/v, 2t). So FP_y = (v^2+t^2)/|v|. Since FP_x^2 = v^2+t^2 and FV = |v|, this is the mean proportional relation FP_x^2 = FV·FP_y, for every P_x on h1. (4) H is the foot of P_y on j, so VH = t^2/|v| = (FP_x^2 − FV^2)/FV. HJ ∥ GE ∥ AV, and AV bisects the angle between the tangents j and g at V. A line parallel to that bisector makes an isosceles triangle VHJ, so VJ = VH, with J on the extension of EV beyond V. Hence EJ = EV + VJ = FV + (FP_x^2 − FV^2)/FV = FP_x^2/FV = FP_y. Combining (2)–(4): EJ = FP_y = KP_y.", "closes_goal": true}
