```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV · FPy for every P_x on h1. Equivalently, F–P_x is the mean proportional between F–V and F–P_y: FV/FPx = FPx/FPy. As a corollary, P_y lies on the parabola with focus F and directrix e.", "reason": "1) GF is a diameter. The tangent f is perpendicular to GA, and j is parallel to f, so j is perpendicular to GF at F. That makes j the tangent to c at F. So V = g ∩ j is where the tangents at E and F meet, which gives VF = VE. 2) Because GF is a diameter, angle GEF = 90°, so FE is perpendicular to line ED (D lies on GE). In right triangle FED, the point V on the hypotenuse FD satisfies VF = VE, so V is the circumcenter, i.e. the midpoint of FD. Hence FD = 2·FV (numerically FV ≈ 2.309 and FD ≈ 4.619). 3) h1 and e are both perpendicular to j, at V and at D. By the intercept theorem on the ray from F, FK = 2·FPx, so P_x is the midpoint of FK. 4) The line m is perpendicular to FK at its midpoint P_x, so m is the perpendicular bisector of FK and P_yF = P_yK. Since KP_y is parallel to j, angle P_yKF = angle KFV (alternate angles). Triangle FKP_y is isosceles, so angle P_yFK = angle P_yKF. Thus FP_x makes the same angle φ with FV and with FP_y. 5) Angle FVP_x = 90° (h1 ⊥ j), so FV = FPx·cos φ. Angle FP_xP_y = 90° (m ⊥ FK), so FPx = FPy·cos φ. Dividing the two gives FV/FPx = FPx/FPy, i.e. FPx^2 = FV·FPy. 6) In coordinates with F = (0,0), j the x-axis, V = (a,0) and P_x = (a,p): K = (2a, 2p) and P_y = (a − p²/a, 2p). Then FP_y = (a²+p²)/a, which equals the distance from P_y to e (x = 2a), so P_y is on the parabola with focus F and directrix e. Also FV·FP_y = a²+p² = FP_x². Numeric check: FPx² ≈ 26.20 and FV·FPy ≈ 2.3094·11.345 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV · FPy for every P_x on h1. Equivalently, FP_x is the mean proportional between FV and FP_y, because triangles F V P_x and F P_x P_y are similar (right angles at V and at P_x, and ray F P_x bisects angle V F P_y).", "reason": "1) j is the tangent at F. GF is a diameter and f ⟂ GF, so the parallel j through F is also ⟂ GF. 2) V is the midpoint of FD. V lies on the tangents at F and E, so VF = VE. Since GF is a diameter, angle GEF = 90°, so FE ⟂ GD. Triangle FED is therefore right-angled at E, and V is a point on its hypotenuse FD with VE = VF. That makes V the midpoint of FD, so FD = 2·FV. 3) Set coordinates with F = (0,0), j as the x-axis, V = (v,0), D = (2v,0). Then h1 is x = v, e is x = 2v, and P_x = (v,t). The ray F P_x meets e at K = (2v,2t), so P_y has height 2t. 4) Let φ be angle V F P_x, so FV = FPx·cosφ and t = FPx·sinφ. P_y lies on the perpendicular to F P_x at P_x, so FPx = FPy·cosψ, where ψ is angle P_x F P_y. Taking ψ = φ, so that P_y is at angle 2φ from j, gives P_y a height of FPy·sin2φ = (FPx/cosφ)·2sinφcosφ = 2t, which matches step 3. So ψ = φ. 5) Hence the right triangles F V P_x (right angle at V) and F P_x P_y (right angle at P_x) share the angle φ at F and are similar. This gives FV/FPx = FPx/FPy, i.e. FPx^2 = FV·FPy, independent of t. Numerical check: FV ≈ 2.3094, FPy ≈ 11.345, product ≈ 26.20, and FPx^2 = 1.7355^2 + 4.8154^2 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FPx^2 = FV · FPy for every P_x on h1, so FPx is the mean proportional between FV and FPy. Equivalently, the right triangles F-V-Px and F-Px-Py are similar, and ray F→Px bisects angle VFPy.", "reason": "(1) GF is a diameter, so the tangent f at G is parallel to the tangent at F. Hence j, the line through F parallel to f, is the tangent to c at F, and V = g ∩ j is where the tangents at E and F meet. That gives VE = VF. (2) GF is a diameter, so angle GEF = 90°, and triangle FED (D = GE ∩ j) is right-angled at E. V lies on the hypotenuse FD and is equidistant from F and E, so V is the midpoint of FD: FD = 2·FV. (3) Take coordinates with F = (0,0), j as the x-axis and V = (v,0). Then D = (2v,0), e is x = 2v, and h1 is x = v. For P_x = (v,t), the ray F→P_x meets e at K = (2v,2t). The line through K parallel to j is y = 2t. Let θ be the angle that F→P_x makes with j, so tan θ = t/v. Then P_y, the point of m (perpendicular to FP_x at P_x) with y = 2t, is exactly the point at angle 2θ from j with FPy = FPx / cos θ. So FP_x bisects angle VFPy. (4) Right triangle F-V-Px (right angle at V, since h1 ⊥ j) gives FV = FPx·cos θ. Right triangle F-Px-Py (right angle at Px, since m ⊥ FPx) gives FPx = FPy·cos θ. These triangles share the angle θ at F, so they are similar, and FV/FPx = FPx/FPy, i.e. FPx^2 = FV·FPy. (5) Numeric check: FPx^2 ≈ 26.20 and FV·FPy ≈ 2.309 × 11.345 ≈ 26.20.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y for every P_x on h1. Equivalently, triangles F V P_x and F P_x P_y are similar, with right angles at V and at P_x.", "reason": "Step 1, V is the midpoint of FD. GF is a diameter, so the tangent at F is perpendicular to GF. The tangent f at G is also perpendicular to GF, so j (through F, parallel to f) is the tangent at F. Since GF is a diameter, angle GEF = 90°. D lies on line GE, so triangle FED has a right angle at E. V is the meet of the tangents at F and E, so VF = VE. A point on the hypotenuse FD that is equidistant from F and E must be the circumcenter of the right triangle, so V is the midpoint of FD and FD = 2·FV. Step 2, coordinates. Put F at the origin with j as the x-axis. Then V = (v, 0) and D = (2v, 0). Lines e and h1 are perpendicular to j, so e is x = 2v and h1 is x = v. Write P_x = (v, t). The ray F→P_x meets e at K = (2v, 2t). So P_y lies on y = 2t. Line m (perpendicular to FP_x at P_x) is v·x + t·y = v² + t². Substituting y = 2t gives P_y = ((v² − t²)/v, 2t). Step 3, the length. FP_y² = [(v² − t²)² + 4v²t²]/v² = (v² + t²)²/v². So FP_y = FP_x²/v = FP_x²/FV, that is, FP_x² = FV·FP_y. Step 4, the geometric reading. FP_y makes twice the angle with j that FP_x makes, so angle VFP_x = angle P_xFP_y. Together with the right angles at V and P_x, this makes triangle FVP_x similar to triangle FP_xP_y, giving FV/FP_x = FP_x/FP_y. Numerical check: FV ≈ 2.309, FP_x ≈ 5.119, FP_y ≈ 11.345, and FV·FP_y ≈ 26.20 ≈ FP_x². Points H and J are not needed for this relation.", "closes_goal": true}
```

